<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wanyan Yongji</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1213">1213</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Prince Weishao</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wanyan Yongji, known by his posthumous title Weishao Wang (Prince of Weishao), was emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchen-jin-dynasty">Jurchen Jin dynasty</a> from 1208 to 1213. A son of the emperor Shizong, he faced the first great Mongol invasion under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, whose victory at Yehuling in 1211 exposed the weakness of Jin defences. In 1213 he was murdered in a coup led by the general Hushahu, and was later denied the title of emperor.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wanyan Yongji, whose childhood name was Xingsheng, was born Wanyan Yunji; his given name was changed to avoid a naming taboo, since the father of Emperor Zhangzong was Wanyan Yungong. He was the seventh son of Emperor Shizong and of Lady Li, a Balhae concubine, and so an uncle of Zhangzong. In 1171 he was made Prince of Xue, and his title was later changed in turn to Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a>, Prince of Lu, Prince of Han and Prince of Wei. When Zhangzong died without a male heir in 1208, he was chosen as the new emperor, the seventh of the Jin dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> were then preparing to attack the Jin. They first struck Western Xia, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Jin; Wanyan Yongji ignored its request for help, and it surrendered. In 1211 the Mongols launched a full-scale invasion and in the ninth month besieged the central capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu">Zhongdu</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, but withdrew before its strong defences. In 1212 they besieged the western capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Datong</a>, while the Khitan Yelü Liuge led a rebellion, submitted to the Mongols and defeated a 600,000-strong Jin army at Dijinao'er. The emperor was known as indecisive and weak.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the eighth month of 1213, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> again attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu">Zhongdu</a>, the general Heshilie Zhizhong, also known as Hushahu, rebelled and killed him; he died on 11 September 1213. Hushahu placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xuanzong-of-jin">Wanyan Xun</a>, Emperor Xuanzong, on the throne and, backed by about 300 officials, urged that the dead ruler be demoted to a commoner. As a compromise Xuanzong made him Marquis of Donghai Commandery. A month later Hushahu was himself killed by the general Zhuhu Gaoqi. In 1216 Xuanzong restored the title Prince of Wei with the posthumous name Shao. One of his daughters, the Princess of Qi State, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wanyan Yongji” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mongol armies ravaged North China, a coup killed the emperor <b>Weishao Wang</b> in 1213, and his successor Xuanzong bought a truce in 1214 with tribute and a princess, then moved the court to Kaifeng.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1203" aria-label="Beijing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7418 736 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Wanyan Yongji"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7868 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7868 1006h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1203">What was here in 1203? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
